---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const breadcrumbs = [
{ label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' },
{ label: 'Pet Nutrition by Age', url: '/en/blog/pet-nutrition-by-age/' }
];
const siteUrl = "https://www.petaginghub.com";
const hrefLangAlternates = [
{ lang: 'zh-TW', url: siteUrl + '/blog/pet-nutrition-by-age' },
{ lang: 'en', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/pet-nutrition-by-age' },
{ lang: 'x-default', url: siteUrl + '/blog/pet-nutrition-by-age' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Pet Nutrition by Life Stage - Pet Aging Hub"
description="What your dog or cat needs to eat changes dramatically from puppyhood to senior years. Here's a complete guide to pet nutrition by age."
lang="en" breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} hrefLangAlternates={hrefLangAlternates}>
<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-4">
<header class="mb-8">
<span class="inline-block bg-green-100 text-green-700 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full mb-4">Nutrition</span>
<h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">Pet Nutrition by Life Stage</h1>
<p class="text-xl text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Nutrition planning should follow your pet’s growth, body and muscle condition, appetite, activity and medical history—not a universal age-based prescription.</p>
</header>

<section class="mb-10">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Dogs</h2>
<div class="space-y-4">
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Puppy</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Choose a complete and balanced food appropriate for growth and, where relevant, large-breed growth. Discuss portions and meal patterns with your veterinary team; do not add minerals or supplements without advice.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Adult</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">For an adult dog, check that the food is complete and balanced for adult maintenance. Use activity, appetite and body-condition trends to review portions with your veterinary team. <a href="/en/blog/pet-exercise-by-age/" class="text-blue-600 underline">exercise for your pet's age</a></p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Senior</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Older dogs can gain weight, lose muscle or have medical needs that change feeding decisions. Review Body Condition Score, Muscle Condition Score, weight trend and history; age alone does not call for a fixed calorie cut or low-phosphorus diet. <a href="/en/blog/senior-dog-care/" class="text-blue-600 underline">senior dog care guide</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="mb-10">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Cats</h2>
<div class="space-y-4">
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Kitten</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Use a complete and balanced kitten diet suitable for growth. Ask your veterinary team about portions and meal patterns, and monitor growth, appetite and body condition rather than relying on a fixed protein range.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Adult</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">For an adult cat, assess nutritional adequacy, preferences, water intake and body condition together. Wet and dry formats can both be suitable; food format alone is not a medical treatment.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-5 border border-slate-200">
<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Senior</h3>
<p class="text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">For an older cat, track appetite, weight and muscle condition rather than prescribing a fixed protein percentage. Changes deserve veterinary discussion, and therapeutic diets should be selected for a medical indication. <a href="/en/blog/senior-cat-care/" class="text-blue-600 underline">senior cat care guide</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="mb-10">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Supplements Worth Considering</h2>
<p class="text-slate-700">Supplements are not automatically needed with a complete and balanced diet. Evidence depends on the product, species and condition. Ask your veterinarian about potential benefits, contraindications and interactions; do not use this page to select a dose.</p>
</section>

<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4">

<a href="/en/blog/senior-pet-health-guide/" class="px-5 py-3 bg-slate-100 hover:bg-slate-200 text-slate-800 font-semibold rounded-xl text-sm">Senior Pet Health Guide</a>
</div>
</article>

<p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-600">Sources: <a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2023-aaha-senior-care-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/nutrition/" class="underline">AAHA: diet and body condition</a>; <a href="https://wsava.org/global-guidelines/global-nutrition-guidelines/" class="underline">WSAVA nutritional assessment and food selection</a>. These support individualized assessment, not fixed nutrient or calorie prescriptions for every senior pet.</p>
<section class="mt-8"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><details class="mb-3"><summary>What should I feed my senior dog?</summary><p class="mt-2">Choose a complete and balanced diet appropriate for the species and growth or adult-maintenance needs. Review body condition, muscle condition, weight trend, appetite, activity and medical history with your veterinarian. Older age alone does not prescribe a calorie cut, fixed protein percentage or low-phosphorus diet.</p></details><details class="mb-3"><summary>Do senior cats need more or less protein?</summary><p class="mt-2">Choose a complete and balanced diet appropriate for the species and growth or adult-maintenance needs. Review body condition, muscle condition, weight trend, appetite, activity and medical history with your veterinarian. Older age alone does not prescribe a calorie cut, fixed protein percentage or low-phosphorus diet.</p></details><details class="mb-3"><summary>When should I switch my puppy to adult food?</summary><p class="mt-2">The transition depends on growth and the individual dog, including breed and expected adult size. Ask your veterinarian when an adult-maintenance diet is appropriate; do not change food solely on a fixed birthday.</p></details><details class="mb-3"><summary>Is wet food or dry food better for cats?</summary><p class="mt-2">Wet and dry foods can both be appropriate when nutritionally complete and suitable for the cat. Moisture, intake, preferences and medical needs matter. Wet food alone is not a treatment for kidney disease; therapeutic diets should be veterinarian-guided when indicated.</p></details></section>
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
"@context": "https://schema.org",
"@type": "FAQPage",
"mainEntity": [
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "What should I feed my senior dog?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Choose a complete and balanced diet appropriate for the species and growth or adult-maintenance needs. Review body condition, muscle condition, weight trend, appetite, activity and medical history with your veterinarian. Older age alone does not prescribe a calorie cut, fixed protein percentage or low-phosphorus diet."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Do senior cats need more or less protein?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Choose a complete and balanced diet appropriate for the species and growth or adult-maintenance needs. Review body condition, muscle condition, weight trend, appetite, activity and medical history with your veterinarian. Older age alone does not prescribe a calorie cut, fixed protein percentage or low-phosphorus diet."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "When should I switch my puppy to adult food?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "The transition depends on growth and the individual dog, including breed and expected adult size. Ask your veterinarian when an adult-maintenance diet is appropriate; do not change food solely on a fixed birthday."
}
},
{
"@type": "Question",
"name": "Is wet food or dry food better for cats?",
"acceptedAnswer": {
"@type": "Answer",
"text": "Wet and dry foods can both be appropriate when nutritionally complete and suitable for the cat. Moisture, intake, preferences and medical needs matter. Wet food alone is not a treatment for kidney disease; therapeutic diets should be veterinarian-guided when indicated."
}
}
]
})} />

</BaseLayout>
